      "name": null,
      "problem": "Expressed as a nonnegative integer smaller than $14,$ the additive inverse of $18$ modulo $14$ is{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The additive inverse of $18$ modulo $14$ is the integer $0\\le x<14$ such that\n\n$$\nx+18≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,14).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note that $18≡4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,14):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18 & ≡1\\cdot 14+4 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,14) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 0+4 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,14) \\\\  & ≡4 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,14)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, note that $-4+4≡0,$ so $x≡-4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,14).$ To write this in the required form, we use the following sequence of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ≡-4 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,14) \\\\  & ≡0-4 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,14) \\\\  & ≡14-4 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,14) \\\\  & ≡10 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,14)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $18$ modulo $14$ is $10.$\n\nWe can verify this result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18+10≡28≡2\\cdot 14≡0\\,\\,✓\\,(\\text{mod}\\,14)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** In general, the additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n,$ i.e. $-a\\,\\text{mod}\\,n.$",
